  <conditions attached="1">
   <condition type="1">
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Consistent with the Article III of the SBAA [or the Supplemental Provisions to the Project Document], the responsibility for the safety and security of the Implementing Partner and its personnel and property, and of UNDP&#x2019;s property in the Implementing Partner&#x2019;s custody, rests with the Implementing Partner.  To this end, the Implementing Partner shall: a)	put in place an appropriate security plan and maintain the security plan, taking into account the security situation in the country where the project is being carried; b)	assume all risks and liabilities related to the Implementing Partner&#x2019;s security, and the full implementation of the security plan.</narrative>
   </condition>
   <condition type="1">
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Social and environmental sustainability will be enhanced through application of the UNDP Social and Environmental Standards (http://www.undp.org/ses) and related Accountability Mechanism (http://www.undp.org/secu-srm).</narrative>
   </condition>
  </conditions>
  <result aggregation-status="1" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Annual Output Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator ascending="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output Indicator</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">1 - Creation of first Angolan MPA approved by Government of Angola on basis of existing legislation with clear defined;
2 - At least 150,000 hectares of new MPA formally established expanding marine species protection;
3 - At least 300 of 550 households practicing sustainable marine resource use based on agreed capture targets and species composition.</narrative>
    </description>
    <baseline year="2019">
     <comment>
      <narrative xml:lang="EN">1 - Law of Biological Aquatic Resources provides overarching framework for MPAs, but lack clear criteria and institutional;
2 - No MPAs established in Angola;
3 - Little of no sustainable marine resource use practices.</narrative>
     </comment>
    </baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2020-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. MPA Management Unit and National Coastal and Marine Unit staff hired
2. National marine conservation and management strategy contracted
3. Study for the creation of marine protected area contracted
4. Monitoring plan for MPA elaborated</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. MPA Management Unit in Namibe hired and office ready in Tombwa; National Coastal and Marine Unit staff hired and working in Luanda
2. National marine conservation and management strategy contracted and initiated
3. Study for the creation of marine protected area contracted and initiated
4. Monitoring plan for MPA elaborated and shared</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2021-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. Marine conservation governance mechanisms are activated thanks to at least 2 cross-sector meeting held, 2 local technical committee held , at least 1 steering committee held and at least 1 transboundary meeting (biomonitoring and law enforcement) held
2. Project communication plan is designed and implemented
3. Management plan for proposed MPA and Tombwa bay are contracted
4. Management plan implementation activities with communities are contracted</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. Marine Protected Area project steering committee established and convened 2 ordinary meetings in June and December 2021 to review project progress and workplan for 2022. Technical reference group established and providing technical support to INBC on the key project substantive matters (relevant sectors actively involved, namely: Ministry of Agriculture and Fisheries, Ministry of Economy and Planning, and others).

2. Development of project communication plan initiated and implementation to start early 2022.

3. Proposed MPA and Tombwa bay management plan consultancy contracted as planned.

4. Community engagement activities initiated, and management plan implementation differed for early 2022.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2022-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. National Strategy for Coastal and Marine Biodiversity finalized.

2. Institutional mechanism for the marine conservation areas system in Angola established.

3. Proposal for the first Marine Conservation Area in Angola finalized and submitted for key stakeholders consultations (local, municipal, provincial and national levels).

4. Development of T&#xf4;mbwa bay management plan initiated.

5. Creation of a Cross-Border Cooperation Mechanism on the monitoring and patrolling of a Marine Protected Area (exchange visit with South Africa).

6. Conduct a beach transect between Ponta Albina and the mouth of the Cunene river to monitor marine mammals and coastal birds.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
    </period>
   </indicator>
